// C7 conformance corpus (#95): the String->Option<u64> parser, USED. The oracle
// (parse_u64.cert.json) is hand-derived (R-CHAR-3) from .design/basis/09-option-result.md
// AC-4 + 07-strings.md REQ-9. The deliverable: a caller given a valid, in-range digit
// string PROVES it gets Some with the round-trip value (the strengthened parse_u64
// contract: all_digits(s) && in-range ==> result is Some && parse_be(s) == the payload).
// The loud None arms (non-digit / overflow / empty) are the handled-or-loud teeth,
// proven at parse_u64's own verification (tests/golden/lower/parse_u64.verus.rs).

// A valid digit string in range parses to Some of its big-endian value — proven (not
// runtime-checked). `ensures result is Some` discharges from the requires via parse_u64's
// strengthened contract; the round-trip `Some(v) => parse_be(s) == v` follows. A None
// mutant violates `result is Some` given the requires -> mutation-killable -> L3. This is the
// calculator's parse primitive: a valid digit string in range -> Some(value), for all
// such inputs.
fn parse_valid(s: String) -> Option<u64>
  ! pure
  requires all_digits(s) && s.len() >= 1 && parse_be(s) <= 18446744073709551615
  ensures result is Some
  ensures match result { Some(v) => parse_be(s) == v, None => true }
{
  parse_u64(s)
}
